        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Incline Percentage vs Degrees", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is treadmill incline measured in percentage or degrees?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Treadmills use percentage grade, which is the rise divided by the horizontal run, multiplied by 100. Degrees measure the angle itself. They are not the same, and the percentage figure is always the larger number — a 15 per cent incline is only 8.5 degrees, which surprises most people the first time they check.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do you convert treadmill incline percentage to degrees?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Divide the percentage by 100, then take the inverse tangent — degrees equal arctan of grade divided by 100. For 10 per cent that is arctan of 0.10, or 5.71 degrees. Below about 20 per cent a rough shortcut works well enough: divide the percentage by 1.75 to get the approximate angle.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many degrees is a 15 percent treadmill incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "8.53 degrees. Fifteen per cent is the maximum on most home treadmills, and while it sounds modest as an angle it is a serious gradient — considerably steeper than almost any road you would drive on, and hard to sustain at a walk for more than a few minutes untrained.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do treadmills use percentage instead of degrees?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because road and trail gradients are quoted the same way, so a percentage grade translates directly to outdoor terrain. It is also the convention in exercise physiology, where the standard equations for oxygen cost and calorie expenditure take fractional grade as their input rather than an angle.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a 1 percent treadmill incline necessary?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is a reasonable default for runners. The commonly cited research found that a 1 per cent grade brings the energy cost of treadmill running closer to running outdoors at the same pace, because indoors there is no air resistance to overcome. For walking, and at slower running paces, the correction matters much less.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good treadmill incline for walking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three to six per cent gives a meaningful increase in effort while still allowing normal walking mechanics. Above ten per cent most people start to lean into the handrails, which offloads body weight and quietly removes much of the benefit. If you cannot walk the grade with your hands off the rails, it is too steep.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
